From 0c75e1fbfab7ca7e1eaad3a0c24331bcee7853ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alban Gruin <alban@pa1ch.fr>
Date: Sat, 16 May 2026 14:03:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] comballoc: emit a single add after a combined allocation
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

With the current backends, an `Ialloc` is compiled into a sequence of
instructions that ends by adding 8 to the allocator pointer (here, in
RISC-V):

    # Allocation…
    addi rp, s10, 8      # Last Ialloc instruction

When combining allocations into a single `Ialloc`, an `Iadd` operation
is appended to add an offset to the pointer.  Hence, the compiled
code looks like this:

    # Allocation…
    addi rp, s10, 8      # Last Ialloc instruction
    addi rp, rp, offset  # Iadd

This change adds an offset value to the `Ialloc` constructor and removes
the `Iadd`, resulting in code that code looks like this:

    # Allocation…
    addi rp, r15, 8 + offset  # Last Ialloc instruction

There is an edge case in RISC-V where it is possible to construct a
program such that an allocation requires an offset of 2040.  This would
require adding 2048 to the final pointer, which is not possible with a
signed 12-bits immediate.  To cover this uncommon case, this addition is
emitted through `emit_addimm`.

Signed-off-by: Alban Gruin <alban@pa1ch.fr>
